<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry Fielding</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1707">1707</a>–<a href="/years/1754">1754</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry Fielding was an English novelist, playwright and magistrate. His satirical plays helped provoke the Licensing Act of 1737, after which he turned to the novel, writing Joseph Andrews and The History of Tom Jones, a Foundling (1749), comic works that helped shape the English novel. As a Westminster magistrate he founded the Bow Street Runners, an early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> police force.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-fielding.jpg" alt="Henry Fielding" width="230" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fielding.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…after the union: David Hume, Adam Smith, whose Wealth of Nations appeared in 1776, and Adam Ferguson shaped modern philosophy, economics and social science, while Samuel Johnson’s Dictionary (1755) and the novels of Daniel Defoe, Samuel Richardson and <b>Henry Fielding</b> helped establish modern English prose and the novel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Writers as diverse as <b>Henry Fielding</b> and Naguib Mahfouz allude to the Nights by name, and among those it influenced are Jorge Luis Borges, Italo Calvino, Angela Carter, Gustave Flaubert, Goethe, Victor Hugo, Marcel Proust, Alexander Pushkin, Salman Rushdie, Leo Tolstoy and W. B. Yeats.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sharpham, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.14,-2.77&amp;year=1737" aria-label="Sharpham on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4487 391 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry Fielding"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4937 661h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4937 661h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.14,-2.77&amp;year=1737">What was here in 1737? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When I mention religion, I mean the Christian religion; and not only the Christian religion, but the Protestant religion; and not only the Protestant religion, but the Church of England.&#8221;</p><footer>Tom Jones (1749), book 3, chapter 3 &#183; original &#183; spoken by the tutor Thwackum &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6593/pg6593-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…love may again be likened to a disease in this, that when it is denied a vent in one part, it will certainly break out in another.&#8221;</p><footer>Tom Jones (1749), book 4, chapter 12 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6593/pg6593-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;To treat of the effects of love to you, must be as absurd as to discourse on colours to a man born blind…&#8221;</p><footer>Tom Jones (1749), book 6, chapter 1 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6593/pg6593-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
